@node	0.2, 0.3, 0.1, Chapter 0
@section Values, names, and functions

This section covers literals, bindings, functions, closures, higher-order
functions, booleans, comparison operators, conditionals, operator
precedence, and the number model this book uses. Everything here reappears
in @ref{1.1} and nothing else from Kotlin is assumed.

@heading Literals and names

The book's integers are @code{Long}, its inexact reals are @code{Double},
its truth values are @code{Boolean}, and its text is a @code{String}.
Underscores may group digits in any numeric literal, and a string template
splices a value into text:

@example kotlin
486
6.626_070_15e-34
true
"n is $n"
@end example

@noindent
A name is bound with @code{val}, which cannot be reassigned; the mutable
@code{var} exists and has a section of its own (@ref{0.5}). A binding is
not a cell the program later assigns into, which is the substance of what
Scheme's @code{define} does in the book's first programs: it names a value.

@example kotlin
val size = 2L
// => 2

5L + 3L + size
// => 10
@end example

@heading Functions

A function is declared with @code{fun}. An expression body writes the body
after @code{=}; a block body is a braced sequence whose value must be
returned explicitly:

@example kotlin
public fun square(x: Long): Long = x * x

public fun hypotenuseSquared(a: Long, b: Long): Long {
    val a2 = square(a)
    val b2 = square(b)
    return a2 + b2
}

square(21)
// => 441

square(2L + 5L)
// => 49

square(square(3))
// => 81
@end example

@noindent
A function may be declared inside another, where it sees the local names of
its enclosing scope, and a local @code{val} plays the role of Scheme's
@code{let}. One restriction matters from chapter 1 on: local functions
cannot forward-reference, so two mutually recursive local functions must be
hoisted or restructured; @ref{0.3} returns to this.

A lambda is a function value. It closes over the names around it, so
@code{makeAdder} builds a family of functions from one declaration:

@example kotlin
public fun makeAdder(n: Long): (Long) -> Long = { x -> x + n }

val add5 = makeAdder(5L)
val add12 = makeAdder(12L)
add5(1L)
// => 6

add12(1L)
// => 13
@end example

@noindent
The type @code{(Long) -> Long} is a function type: a procedure is an
ordinary value. A @code{typealias} names a shape that recurs, a declaration
can pass functions as arguments, and a call whose last argument is a
function may write it after the parentheses in trailing position:

@example kotlin
public typealias Mapper = (Long) -> Long

public fun sumOver(f: Mapper, a: Long, b: Long): Long =
    (a..b).fold(0L) { acc, k -> acc + f(k) }

public fun twice(m: Mapper): Mapper = { x -> m(m(x)) }

sumOver(::square, 1L, 3L)
// => 14

val quad = twice(::square)
quad(2L)
// => 16
@end example

@noindent
The @code{::square} form is a reference to a named function, usable wherever
a lambda would do. Higher-order programs from @ref{1.3} translate onto this
directly, and the composed procedures of @ref{3.1} are closures of exactly
the @code{makeAdder} shape.

@heading Booleans, comparisons, and conditionals

The comparison operators are @code{== != < > <= >=}, the connectives are
@code{&& || !}, and @code{==} compares values by content for the data types
of @ref{0.4}. Both @code{if} and @code{when} are expressions: they produce
the value of the branch taken, and a @code{when} tries its candidates in
order:

@example kotlin
public fun sign(n: Long): String =
    if (n > 0L) {
        "positive"
    } else if (n < 0L) {
        "negative"
    } else {
        "zero"
    }

public fun classify(n: Long): String =
    when (n) {
        0L -> "zero"
        in 1L..9L -> "small"
        else -> "large"
    }

sign(-3L)
// => negative

classify(7L)
// => small

classify(42L)
// => large
@end example

@noindent
Operator precedence follows the customary infix reading: @code{* /} bind
tighter than @code{+ -}, comparison binds looser than arithmetic, and
@code{&&} binds tighter than @code{||}. Parentheses win, and the book's
listings use them wherever two readings are plausible.

@heading The number model

An integer is a 64-bit @code{Long} and a real is a @code{Double}. The
division rule is the one the book's first sessions need: @code{Long / Long}
truncates toward zero, and real division requires a real operand.

@example kotlin
public fun halfOf(n: Long): Long = n / 2L
public fun realHalf(n: Long): Double = n / 2.0

halfOf(9L)
// => 4

realHalf(9L)
// => 4.5
@end example

@footnote{@code{Long} arithmetic wraps silently when it passes its width,
as on the JVM. Listings that can plausibly overflow call
@code{Math.addExact} and its siblings, which throw instead of wrapping; the
exercises of @ref{1.2} probe that boundary, and the runtime's interpreted
integers use checked arithmetic that raises a typed overflow error
(@ref{0.6}).}

Small counters, indices, and the register pointers of chapter 5 use
@code{Int}; everything the main text prints as a number is a @code{Long} or
a @code{Double}.

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.1}Exercise 0.1:} Below are three of the
interactive sessions of section 1.1 of this book, kept in their Scheme
form. Translate each into Kotlin, evaluating the expressions in order, with
each call rendered in Kotlin and each response written as a
@code{// ;Value:} comment, as the transcripts of this section do. Verify
that every numeric response matches Scheme's.

@example scheme
486
100
(+ 5 3 4)
(- 10 9)
(+ (* 2 4) (- 4 6))
@end example

@example scheme
(define a 3)
(define b (+ a 1))
(+ a b (* a b))
(= a b)
(if (and (> b a) (< b (* a b)))
    b
    a)
(cond ((= a 4) 6)
      ((= b 4) (+ 6 7 a))
      (else (+ 25 a)))
(+ 2 (if (> b a) b a))
(* (cond ((> a b) a)
         ((< a b) b)
         (else -1))
   (+ a 1))
@end example

@example scheme
(define (square x) (* x x))
(square 21)
(square (+ 2 5))
(square (square 3))
@end example

@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.2}Exercise 0.2:} Implement @code{compose} and
@code{repeated} over @code{(Long) -> Long} procedures:
@code{compose(f, g)(x)} applies @code{g} and then @code{f}, and
@code{repeated(f, n)(x)} applies @code{f} exactly @code{n} times, with
@code{repeated(f, 0)(x)} the identity. Prove them with Kotest property
tests: use @code{checkAll} over generated inputs and compare each result
with hand-computed cases, including the @code{n = 0} boundary and both
orders of @code{compose}.
